## Use forge skills to guide your work

Skills come from the binary, so they match the forge you run. Load one before guessing. These five show the most, soonest:

```bash
{{.CLI}} skill load forge                # start here: what forge already scaffolds, so you never hand-write it
{{.CLI}} project shapes --grep <Name>    # where a symbol is in every layer (rpc, table, handler, hook, store), with file:line
{{.CLI}} env up dev                      # the whole stack locally; every log, browser console included, in .forge/logs/dev/
{{.CLI}} obs endpoints dev               # p50/p95/p99 and error rate per RPC, with no instrumentation code
{{.CLI}} obs check <env> --wait --json   # after a deploy: proves the release good, or names what regressed
```

`{{.CLI}} skill list` is the catalog. `{{.CLI}} project libraries <pkg>` prints a forge library's whole API: never search the filesystem for forge's source. `{{.CLI}} project capabilities` lists every verb; `{{.CLI}} project benefits` lists what this project gets without setup. A skill that disagrees with this project is a forge bug: trust the project and report it (below).

## Critical rules

1. **Never edit generated code** — `gen/`, `*_gen.go`, `*-hooks.ts`. Edit the input (the proto, a migration, config, a package's `contract.go`) and run `{{.CLI}} generate`. It never overwrites your files: handlers, `internal/app/providers.go` and `compose.go` are yours (forge only adds new components and Deps keys to compose.go). Parallel runs queue on `.forge/forge.lock`; an unchanged tree says `nothing to do` in seconds.
2. **Proto is the API's canonical input; migrations are the DB source of truth.** Entities and the ORM are projected from the APPLIED `db/migrations` schema, never from proto.
3. **The gate before you call a phase done:**
   ```
   {{.CLI}} generate && {{.CLI}} lint && go build ./...{{if $taskGate}} && task test{{else}}{{range .Gate.Commands}}{{if eq .Does "tests"}} && {{.Run}}{{end}}{{end}}{{end}}
   ```
   Never pipe `{{.CLI}} lint` through `head`: the verdict is the LAST line. `--quiet` prints only what failed; `--scope <path>` lints your slice of a shared checkout (run it unscoped before merging).
{{if $taskGate}}4. **Iterate on `task test:short -- ./internal/<pkg>/...`** (cached; never add `-count=1` or `-race`, never set a private `GOCACHE`), then `task test` once before you finish; `task ci` is the whole PR gate (`ci`). A test over ~2s calls `testkit.Slow(t, "<why>")` first — gate, don't gut (`testing`). `⏳ waiting for a heavy slot` is a queue, not a hang: never kill the holders (`ci/slots`).
{{- else}}4. **This project has no Taskfile, so there is no `task test` and no `{{.CLI}} test`.** {{template "gate-commands" .}} Iterate on `go test -short ./internal/<pkg>/...` (cached; never add `-count=1` or `-race`). A test over ~2s calls `testkit.Slow(t, "<why>")` first — gate, don't gut (`testing`).{{end}}
   Anything over ~2 minutes runs in the background (`run_in_background` + `shell_wait`), never detached with `nohup`, `&`, `setsid` or `disown`.
5. **Debugging: `{{.CLI}} skill load debug` first.** Frontend `console.*` calls and uncaught errors are on disk beside the backend logs: `grep '\[browser:error\]' .forge/logs/dev/*.log`.
6. **`auth_required: false` publishes one RPC, never a page.** The pages a signed-out visitor may open are the frontend route guard's `publicExactPaths` (`src/lib/auth/route-guard.tsx`). What a caller may do — access checks, row scoping — is your handler code (`middleware.GetUser`); forge generates none of it (`auth`).

## Architecture in one screen

This is a Go project. `{{.CLI}} scaffold service <svc>` writes:

```
proto/services/<svc>/v1/<svc>.proto   ← the API: messages, CRUD + custom rpcs
gen/services/<svc>/v1/...             ← generated stubs (never edit)
internal/handlers/<svc>/              ← THE SERVICE, one dir: service.go (Deps, New), rpc_<name>.go
                                        (one custom rpc per file — the logic goes HERE), handlers_crud.go
                                        (CRUD shims), handlers_crud_ops_gen.go (generated)
internal/<pkg>/                       ← optional `{{.CLI}} scaffold package <pkg>`: contract.go + service.go
```

The `*Service` methods ARE the handlers; they reach `internal/db/` and other collaborators through `s.deps`. Load `architecture` for the full tree, and `service-layer` for the package-boundary rules.
{{- if .Unmanaged}}

**Services forge does not manage:** {{range $i, $s := .Unmanaged}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}`{{$s}}`{{end}} (`// forge:unmanaged`).
Their handlers stay where this project already keeps them: buf still
generates their code, forge scaffolds nothing for them, and the layout above
does not apply to them. Never move one into `internal/handlers/` unless the
user asks; adopting a service means removing its marker, one service per PR
(`migration` skill). `{{.CLI}} project audit` lists them.
{{- end}}

## Where logic goes

| Concern | Lives in | Skill |
|---------|----------|-------|
| An rpc's logic | the `*Service` method in `rpc_<name>.go` (CRUD: the shim in `handlers_crud.go`) | `api` |
| Errors crossing the rpc boundary | `return nil, svcerr.Wrap(err)` — never a per-service `connect.Code` switch | `api` |
| Logic a worker or another service shares, or worth transport-free tests | `{{.CLI}} scaffold package <name>`, taken as a Deps interface | `service-layer` |
| Read-check-write transactions | `s.deps.DB.RunTx(ctx, orm.Serializable, …)` — every transaction names its level (`orm.ReadCommitted` only when a lock makes it correct) | `service-layer/transactions` |
| Wiring | `internal/app/providers.go` and `compose.go` (both yours) | `services` |
| DB schema | `db/migrations/` | `db` |

## Proto and schema rules

- An rpc is CRUD only when unary and named exactly `Create<E>`/`Get<E>`/`Update<E>`/`Delete<E>`/`List<Es>` for an entity (a table AND a message). Everything else is custom and yours. A CRUD-named rpc that does more (`CreateTask` also wires dependencies) gets `// forge:custom` above it; never rename one to dodge detection (`proto`).
- Behavior comes from real columns: `created_at`+`updated_at` ⇒ managed timestamps, `deleted_at` ⇒ soft delete. The old proto entity annotations are retired and ignored: delete leftovers. Fields clients must not write: `db/write-policy`.
- List filter fields **must** be `optional` (otherwise they silently no-op); one service per `.proto` file; shared messages in `proto/shared/v1/types.proto`, never service-to-service imports. `{{.CLI}} lint --conventions` enforces these.

## The scaffolded frontend is starter code

The dashboard's entity tiles, the sidebar nav, the sign-in screen and every generated list/detail/create/edit page prove the stack is wired; they are not a design. They are yours, and rewriting them is expected. Forge withholds a visual identity on purpose — an obviously neutral scaffold is easier to replace than an invented taste. Before showing any of it to a user, load `frontend/design`: it asks the user for a brief first.

## Shipping

`{{.CLI}} env deploy <env>` ships to Reliant hosting (staging/prod are already bound to it; `deploy`), then `{{.CLI}} obs check <env> --wait --json` proves it. One hosted static site per person is free, counted across environments (each env's site is one); anything beyond it needs billing, and until then `env deploy` exits 7 and prints the billing link: give the user that link, don't retry. Never ask for `forge login` under Reliant. Not signed in at all? `--claimable` deploys with no signup and prints a claim link for the user (`deploy/hosting`).

{{end -}}
## When forge itself is wrong — FIX IT

If forge emits something wrong — bad codegen, a lint false positive, a scaffold quirk, a missing capability — fix it in forge when you can; never paper over it or hand-edit generated output, which the next `{{.CLI}} generate` overwrites. forge is semver at major 1: a fix keeps the old flag, field or default working beside the new one, marked deprecated. When you cannot fix it here, report it and carry on:

    {{.CLI}} report-bug --severity high --title "<one line naming the defect>" \
      --actual "<the exact error text>" --evidence "<commands, output, file paths>"

Only for defects in forge itself (a command, codegen, lint, skill or doc), not this project's code or tests. Never put credentials or file contents in a report; `--dry-run` shows exactly what would be sent.

