## Environments and deploys

The envs are the declaration: `deploy/kcl/<env>/main.k`. Change a binding or an env value there, then check it with `{{.CLI}} env render <env>`. `deploy`, `deploy/hosting` and `secrets` are the skills.

```bash
{{.CLI}} env up dev                     # run dev as declared{{if or .Workloads .Container}}: the app's dev command as a host process, with PORT (and DATABASE_URL) set{{end}}
{{.CLI}} env render <env>               # check an env's declaration
{{.CLI}} env deploy <env>               # build ONCE, push, cut a release, promote it
{{.CLI}} obs check <env> --wait --json  # prove the release good; branch on its exit code
{{.CLI}} env deploy <next-env> <release> # promote the same bytes, by digest — no rebuild
```
{{if or .Workloads .Container}}
The dev command is the `command` in `deploy/kcl/dev/main.k`: replace it with your watch / hot-reload loop.
{{end}}
A hosted deploy needs billing on the Reliant organization; until it is set up `env deploy` exits 7 and prints the billing link. Give the user that link; don't retry.

- The app reads its config from environment variables. A per-env value is `env` on the app in that env's `main.k`; a credential is `env = {KEY = forge.ManagedSecret {name = "KEY"}}` there, with its value set once by `{{.CLI}} secret set --env <env> KEY` (stdin). forge never sees a secret's value.
- A managed database (if declared) reaches the app as `DATABASE_URL` through `forge.DatabaseRef`.
- `{{.CLI}} obs logs <env>` and `{{.CLI}} obs workloads <env>` (restarts, crash loops, failed jobs) work for any app. Request rates, error rates and traces exist only if the app exports OpenTelemetry (OTLP to `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`, which hosting sets on every workload): until it does, `{{.CLI}} obs check` is INCONCLUSIVE with `reason_code: no_request_telemetry` — not proven good — while restarts, alerts and issues still count. Load `observability/query` for the details.
